      "id": "ED-014",
      "body_markdown": "**Source:** The cello sul ponticello passage uses historical treble notation interpreted one octave below its written pitches. It begins at V.469 and continues through the first note of V.487, after which bass clef resumes.\n\n**Edition:** Tenor clef represents the intended sounding octave. The opening figure sounds B4–G-sharp4–E4–G-sharp4. Bass clef resumes after the first quarter note of V.487.\n\n**Editorial reason:** Adopt modern cello octave notation under C2.3.1 and C4. The sounding pitches are retained; this is a notation change, not an octave transposition of the music.",
